{"i":4,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"plan.json\", \"pointer\":…10249 tokens truncated…ge of such an expression is c0/d0; a zero image implies c0=0 and hence a zero tensor. Thus multiplication is injective and identifies QJ with the rational span of J.\"}, {\"id\": \"S013\", \"text\": \"Let r=dim_ℚ QJ. Apply M002 with source B=R, A=ℚ, C=M_R. The rings R and ℚ are nontrivial commutative domains and satisfy the strong rank condition: an injective finite matrix over R remains injective over ℚ by clearing denominators of a hypothetical kernel vector, and the dimension inequality over ℚ then gives the required rank inequality; the inclusion R→ℚ gives the algebra structure. N002 states that M_R is free of rank n, so M002 gives dim_ℚ(ℚ⊗_R M_R)=n. Tensoring the coordinate isomorphism of N003 gives ℚ⊗_R M_R≅∏_{i∈I}QJ: on a pure tensor with coordinates (gamma_i), the map sends ρ⊗(∑_{i∈I}gamma_i e_i) to (ρ⊗gamma_i)_{i∈I}; inverse maps insert the coordinates and sum. These inverse maps are balanced and linear, so this identification needs no flatness assumption. Apply M001 with A=ℚ, B=I and constant family C_i=QJ. Here I is finite with |I|=n by N001; QJ is a finite-dimensional vector space by F001, hence free and finitely generated. We obtain dim_ℚ(∏_{i∈I}QJ)=∑_{i∈I}r=nr. Therefore n=nr. Since n>0 by N001, r=1.\"}, {\"id\": \"S014\", \"text\": \"Choose 0≠a∈J and a finite nonempty tuple g_1,…,g_k of nonzero generators of J, as permitted by N003. By F001 and F002, J lies in a one-dimensional rational line, and its nonzero vector a spans that line. Consequently each g_j=q_j a with a nonzero rational q_j. This proves the rational-coordinate description of a generating set; it is derived from the rank calculation rather than assumed merely from J⊂ℂ.\"}, {\"id\": \"S015\", \"text\": \"For each nonzero rational q_j, strip the factors p from its integer numerator and denominator. Since p is prime by H001, this gives q_j=p^(eta_j)u_j, with eta_j=ν_j∈ℤ and both numerator and denominator of u_j prime to p. Thus u_j and u_j^(-1) are in R. Put ν=min_j eta_j, which exists for the nonempty finite tuple of F003, and b=p^ν a≠0. Every generator q_j a=p^(eta_j−ν)u_j b belongs to Rb because eta_j−ν≥0. Conversely, for any generator attaining the minimum, multiplication of that generator by its unit u_j^(-1) gives b; hence b∈J and Rb⊆J. The first inclusion on generators gives J⊆Rb, so J=Rb.\"}, {\"id\": \"S016\", \"text\": \"N003 now gives M_R=⊕_{i∈I}Rb e_i. Its coordinate sum map shows that (b e_i) spans over R; since b≠0 and the e family is complex-linearly independent by N001, it is R-linearly independent. Thus it is an R-basis. The same reasoning works for any nonzero b with J=Rb. Together with F001 and F002 this proves the asserted rank and basis conclusions, exporting existence of such b without fixing its private identity.\"}, {\"id\": \"S017\", \"text\": \"By H001, every integer prime to p has nonzero image in K and therefore an inverse. The formula defining kappa:R→K is independent of fractional representatives by cross multiplication, preserves addition and multiplication, and extends the integer map. The balanced tensor maps V_K=M⊗_ℤ K→M_R⊗_R K and back are m⊗c↦(1⊗m)⊗c and (s⊗m)⊗c↦m⊗kappa(s)c. The tensor relations show that both are well-defined; the two composites fix pure tensors, hence are identity maps. Thus they are inverse K-linear isomorphisms. Since the maps act only on scalar factors, they intertwine every operator preserving M, including x,y,h,X_r,Y_r by N002.\"}, {\"id\": \"S018\", \"text\": \"The reduction homomorphism R→F_p sends an integer fraction to numerator times inverse denominator. It is surjective because integers already map onto F_p. Its kernel is pR: a reduced fraction maps to zero exactly when its numerator is divisible by p, and division of that numerator by p stays in R. Consequently R/pR≅F_p and M_R/pM_R is an F_p-module. The maps M_R⊗_R K→(M_R/pM_R)⊗_(F_p)K and back send u⊗c to [u]⊗c and [u]⊗c to u⊗c. Changing u by a multiple of p changes u⊗c by zero, since p=0 in K. These formulas are balanced for the quotient R-action, are independent of representatives, and are inverse on pure tensors. They give the second K-linear isomorphism and commute with all induced lattice operators. Combine with F001 to obtain both tensor identifications asserted in the output.\"}, {\"id\": \"S019\", \"text\": \"Choose b≠0 in J with J=Rb using N004. The family (b e_i)_{i∈I} is an R-basis of M_R by N004, using iota from N002. Define beta_i to be its reduced image in V_K through F001. Scalar extension of its coordinate maps gives a K-basis: every pure tensor has its finite coordinate expansion in beta_i, and the extended coordinate maps take beta_i to the corresponding coordinate unit vectors, proving independence. Therefore dim_K V_K=|I|=n=λ+1>0 by N001 and H002. Applying N001 to b e_i and using the tensor isomorphisms gives x beta_0=0, x beta_i=(λ−i+1)beta_(i−1) for 1≤i≤λ; y beta_i=(i+1)beta_(i+1) for i<λ, y beta_λ=0; h beta_i=(λ−2i)beta_i. All coefficients are the integer images in K. This proves the required basis and its generator matrices.\"}, {\"id\": \"S020\", \"text\": \"For each r∈I, r≤λ<p by H002, so r! is nonzero in K by H001; for r=0 its value is 1. On the lattice, r!X_r=x^r and r!Y_r=y^r; these equalities extend to V_K. Thus X_r=(r!)^(-1)x^r and Y_r=(r!)^(-1)y^r on V_K for 0≤r≤λ. By N002, each E_ij preserves M_R; let T_ij be its induced operator on V_K. Its action on beta is T_ij beta_k=δ_jk beta_i. The identities in N002 extend through F001: binom(λ,j) is a unit of R, so its image in K is a unit, and T_ij=binom(λ,j)^(-1)X_(λ−i)(Y_λ X_λ)Y_(λ−j). This is exactly the original matrix-unit identity reduced on the beta basis. All its divided-power indices lie between 0 and λ, so the factorial inverses just established apply. Taking b_i=beta_i in the existential output, F002 and F003 complete all the asserted reduction and basis formulas.\"}, {\"id\": \"S021\", \"text\": \"Choose the basis beta supplied by N005 and define T by its displayed formula. Let W⊆V_K be any K-linear subspace. Assume L1: W is invariant under x,y,h. Closure under x,y implies closure under their nonnegative powers. The divided-power indices in T_ij are λ−i,λ,λ−j, all within 0,…,λ; N005 identifies the corresponding X,Y with scalar multiples of those powers. A linear subspace is closed under scalar multiplication and compositions of preserving maps, so W is invariant under every T_ij.\"}, {\"id\": \"S022\", \"text\": \"In the zero case temporarily assume L2: W={0}. Then the desired disjunction W={0} or W=V_K follows immediately. Discharge L2 to obtain the implication for this case.\"}, {\"id\": \"S023\", \"text\": \"For the other case assume L3: W≠{0}. A nonzero subspace contains a nonzero vector, and the beta basis of N005 expands any such vector uniquely. Its coefficients cannot all be zero. Thus there exist a vector w∈W, a coefficient family c∈K^I and an index j∈I with w=∑_{k∈I}c_k beta_k, w≠0 and c_j≠0. Record this compatible joint existence before choosing the witnesses.\"}, {\"id\": \"S024\", \"text\": \"Choose compatible w,c,j as in F003. For arbitrary i∈I, the matrix-unit action of N005 gives T_ij w=c_j beta_i. By F001 this vector belongs to W. Since c_j≠0 in the field K, multiplication by c_j^(-1) gives beta_i∈W. This holds for every i; since beta is a K-basis, W=V_K. No chosen vector or coordinate is present in that conclusion. Discharge L3 to obtain: if W≠{0}, then W=V_K.\"}, {\"id\": \"S025\", \"text\": \"Classical case elimination on W={0} or W≠{0}, using F002 and F004, gives W={0} or W=V_K. Discharge L1 and universally quantify the arbitrary W: every K-linear subspace invariant under x,y,h is zero or V_K. N005 also gives dim_K V_K=λ+1>0, so V_K is nonzero. By H003 and the definition of L_K, x,y,h span its lattice-induced action; invariance under those generators is equivalent to L_K-invariance. Thus V(K) is irreducible as an L(K)-module. H001 and H002 give characteristic p and 0≤λ<p throughout these constructions. For λ=0, every factorial and binomial used is 1 and the same argument gives a one-dimensional space. If p=2, the permitted values are λ=0,1 and the proof still uses no distinct reduced h-eigenvalues. No step required K to be algebraically closed.\"}], \"paragraph_mapping\": {\"S001\": {\"inference\": \"R001\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S002\": {\"inference\": \"R001\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S003\": {\"inference\": \"R001\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S004\": {\"inference\": \"R001\", \"paragraph\": \"S004\"}, \"S005\": {\"inference\": \"R001\", \"paragraph\": \"S005\"}, \"S006\": {\"inference\": \"R002\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S007\": {\"inference\": \"R002\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S008\": {\"inference\": \"R002\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S009\": {\"inference\": \"R003\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S010\": {\"inference\": \"R003\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S011\": {\"inference\": \"R003\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S012\": {\"inference\": \"R004\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S013\": {\"inference\": \"R004\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S014\": {\"inference\": \"R004\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S015\": {\"inference\": \"R004\", \"paragraph\": \"S004\"}, \"S016\": {\"inference\": \"R004\", \"paragraph\": \"S005\"}, \"S017\": {\"inference\": \"R005\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S018\": {\"inference\": \"R005\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S019\": {\"inference\": \"R005\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S020\": {\"inference\": \"R005\", \"paragraph\": \"S004\"}, \"S021\": {\"inference\": \"R006\", \"paragraph\": \"S001\"}, \"S022\": {\"inference\": \"R006\", \"paragraph\": \"S002\"}, \"S023\": {\"inference\": \"R006\", \"paragraph\": \"S003\"}, \"S024\": {\"inference\": \"R006\", \"paragraph\": \"S004\"}, \"S025\": {\"inference\": \"R006\", \"paragraph\": \"S005\"}}, \"roots\": [{\"id\": \"H001\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"p is a prime number and K has characteristic p.\"}, {\"id\": \"H002\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"λ is an integer satisfying 0≤λ<p.\"}, {\"id\": \"H003\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"V is the complex irreducible highest-weight sl₂(ℂ)-module of highest weight λ, of complex dimension λ+1. The Chevalley generators x,y,h of sl₂(ℂ) act on V and satisfy [h,x]=2x, [h,y]=−2y, [x,y]=h. In its universal enveloping algebra and in the induced operators on V, the bracket is [a,b]=ab−ba; the same symbols denote the corresponding operators.\"}, {\"id\": \"H004\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"v∈V is nonzero, xv=0, and hv=λv.\"}, {\"id\": \"H005\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"M⊂V is an arbitrary admissible lattice: a free abelian subgroup admitting an integer basis which is also a complex basis of V, and stable under U_Z. No minimal-lattice assumption is imposed.\"}, {\"id\": \"M001\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"Let A be a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition (every injective linear map between finite free A-modules has source rank at most target rank), let B be a finite index type, and for each t∈B let C_t be an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure, free and finitely generated over A. Then finrank_A(∏_{t∈B} C_t)=∑_{t∈B} finrank_A(C_t). Here finrank is cardinal module rank converted to a natural number (zero if that cardinal is infinite).\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"Module.finrank_pi_fintype\", \"hypotheses\": [\"A is a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition.\", \"B is finite.\", \"Each C_t is an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure.\", \"Each C_t is free and finitely generated as an A-module.\"], \"statement\": \"Let A be a semiring satisfying the strong rank condition (every injective linear map between finite free A-modules has source rank at most target rank), let B be a finite index type, and for each t∈B let C_t be an additive commutative monoid with an A-module structure, free and finitely generated over A. Then finrank_A(∏_{t∈B} C_t)=∑_{t∈B} finrank_A(C_t). Here finrank is cardinal module rank converted to a natural number (zero if that cardinal is infinite).\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Constructions\", \"signature\": \"@Module.finrank_pi_fintype : ∀ (R : Type u) [inst : Semiring.{u} R] [StrongRankCondition.{u} R] {ι : Type v} [inst_2 : Fintype.{v} ι]\\n  {M : ι → Type w} [inst_3 : (i : ι) → AddCommMonoid.{w} (M i)] [inst_4 : (i : ι) → _root_.Module.{u, w} R (M i)]\\n  [∀ (i : ι), Module.Free.{u, w} R (M i)] [∀ (i : ι), Module.Finite.{u, w} R (M i)],\\n  Eq.{1} (Module.finrank.{u, max v w} R ((i : ι) → M i)) (∑ i, Module.finrank.{u, w} R (M i))\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Dimension/Constructions.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"395f49a423c4c46bb246eb11c785cbc350b3f938b1fa8b7e840635b208953e10\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"library/M001-0001/library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"eee515576db994f4326b0cccef71b92b3d2bf3baa3f2cc5ccfa161ad7729972a\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"M002\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"Let A be a semiring, B a commutative semiring, both satisfying the strong rank condition (an injective linear map between finite free modules has source rank at most target rank), and C an additive commutative monoid with a free B-module structure. Given a B-algebra structure on A, finrank_A(A⊗_B C)=finrank_B(C). Here finrank is cardinal module rank converted to a natural number (zero if infinite); C need not be finitely generated.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"Module.finrank_baseChange\", \"hypotheses\": [\"A is a semiring and B is a commutative semiring, both satisfying the strong rank condition.\", \"C is an additive commutative monoid with a free B-module structure.\", \"A carries a B-algebra structure.\"], \"statement\": \"Let A be a semiring, B a commutative semiring, both satisfying the strong rank condition (an injective linear map between finite free modules has source rank at most target rank), and C an additive commutative monoid with a free B-module structure. Given a B-algebra structure on A, finrank_A(A⊗_B C)=finrank_B(C). Here finrank is cardinal module rank converted to a natural number (zero if infinite); C need not be finitely generated.\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Dimension.Constructions\", \"signature\": \"@Module.finrank_baseChange : ∀ {R : Type u} {S : Type u'} {M' : Type v'} [inst : Semiring.{u} R] [inst_1 : CommSemiring.{u'} S]\\n  [inst_2 : AddCommMonoid.{v'} M'] [StrongRankCondition.{u} R] [StrongRankCondition.{u'} S]\\n  [inst_5 : _root_.Module.{u', v'} S M'] [Module.Free.{u', v'} S M'] [inst_7 : Algebra.{u', u} S R],\\n  Eq.{1} (Module.finrank.{u, max v' u} R (TensorProduct.{u', u, v'} S R M')) (Module.finrank.{u', v'} S M')\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Dimension/Constructions.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"395f49a423c4c46bb246eb11c785cbc350b3f938b1fa8b7e840635b208953e10\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"library/M002-0002/library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"48c51156444fbfd7be7fdb5dc9a492adb9224aa2b702d4c01ecca942cf015806\"}]}}], \"source_outputs\": {\"N001\": \"n=λ+1>0 and |I|=n. For every integer t≥0, h y^t v=(λ−2t)y^t v; for t≥1, x y^t v=t(λ−t+1)y^(t−1)v. The vectors (e_i)_{i∈I} form a complex basis of V, and y^(λ+1)v=0 (hence all higher lowering powers vanish). On this basis, xe_0=0, xe_i=(λ−i+1)e_(i−1) for 1≤i≤λ, ye_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) for 0≤i<λ, ye_λ=0, he_i=(λ−2i)e_i. For all i∈I and natural r, X_r e_i=binom(λ−i+r,r)e_(i−r) if r≤i and zero otherwise; Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r) if i+r≤λ and zero otherwise. All displayed divided-power coefficients are integers.\", \"N002\": \"iota embeds M_R as the R-span of M in V; M_R is free of rank n over R and its complex span is V. Each X_r and Y_r (r≥0) preserves M and M_R, and x,y,h preserve M and M_R. For every i,j∈I, E_ij preserves M_R (under iota). The following identities hold on V: Pi=Y_λ X_λ=E_λλ, X_(λ−i)Pi=E_iλ, Pi Y_(λ−j)=binom(λ,j)E_λj, and E_ij=binom(λ,j)^(-1) X_(λ−i)Pi Y_(λ−j), where binom(λ,j) is a unit in R.\", \"N003\": \"For every i∈I one has J_i=J. The R-module J⊂ℂ is nonzero and finitely generated. The map (c_i)_{i∈I}↦∑_{i∈I}c_i e_i is an R-linear isomorphism ∏_{i∈I}J→iota(M_R), so, identifying M_R through iota, M_R=⊕_{i∈I}J e_i.\", \"N004\": \"The multiplication map ℚ⊗_R J→ℂ is injective and its image is the rational span of J, a one-dimensional ℚ-vector space. There exists b∈J with b≠0 and J=Rb. For any such b, the family (b e_i)_{i∈I} is an R-basis of M_R identified with iota(M_R).\", \"N005\": \"The natural tensor maps give K-linear isomorphisms V_K≅M_R⊗_R K≅(M_R/pM_R)⊗_(F_p)K, intertwining all lattice operators. There exists a K-basis (b_i)_{i∈I} of V_K obtained from an R-basis (b e_i) of M_R with b∈J nonzero. In particular dim_K(V_K)=n=λ+1>0. On this basis the induced operators satisfy xb_0=0, xb_i=(λ−i+1)b_(i−1) for 1≤i≤λ, yb_i=(i+1)b_(i+1) for i<λ, yb_λ=0, hb_i=(λ−2i)b_i, with integer coefficients interpreted in K. For every 0≤r≤λ, r! is nonzero in K and the induced divided powers X_r,Y_r equal (r!)^(-1)x^r,(r!)^(-1)y^r. Every binom(λ,j) for j∈I is nonzero in K. The reduced operators T_ij=binom(λ,j)^(-1) X_(λ−i)(Y_λ X_λ)Y_(λ−j), for i,j∈I, obey T_ij b_k=δ_jk b_i for every k∈I, where δ_jk is 1 for j=k and 0 otherwise.\", \"P\": \"V_K is nonzero, and for every K-linear subspace W⊆V_K, if W is invariant under the induced x,y,h then W={0} or W=V_K. Equivalently V(K) is irreducible as an L(K)-module. This holds for every given prime p, field K of characteristic p, integer 0≤λ<p, and arbitrary admissible lattice M, including λ=0 and p=2, without assuming K algebraically closed.\"}, \"direct_boundaries\": {\"R001\": {\"inputs\": [\"H002\", \"H003\", \"H004\"], \"output\": \"N001\", \"background_notes\": [\"R001.BG001\", \"R001.BG002\"]}, \"R002\": {\"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"H002\", \"H003\", \"H005\", \"N001\"], \"output\": \"N002\", \"background_notes\": [\"R002.BG001\", \"R002.BG002\"]}, \"R003\": {\"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"H002\", \"N001\", \"N002\"], \"output\": \"N003\", \"background_notes\": [\"R003.BG001\"]}, \"R004\": {\"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"H002\", \"N001\", \"N002\", \"N003\", \"M001\", \"M002\"], \"output\": \"N004\", \"background_notes\": [\"R004.BG001\", \"R004.BG002\", \"R004.BG003\", \"R004.BG004\", \"R004.BG005\"]}, \"R005\": {\"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"H002\", \"N001\", \"N002\", \"N004\"], \"output\": \"N005\", \"background_notes\": [\"R005.BG001\", \"R005.BG002\", \"R005.BG003\"]}, \"R006\": {\"inputs\": [\"H003\", \"N005\", \"H001\", \"H002\"], \"output\": \"P\", \"background_notes\": [\"R006.BG001\", \"R006.BG002\", \"R006.BG003\", \"R006.BG004\"]}}}, \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
