{"id":"plan.json","pointer":"","view":"value","value":{"version":2,"contract_sha256":"84f26158cde742dbfac2a828a2dcadf40e9a9c0f9647c7712e9fb31b9f596384","representation_notes":"V is an arbitrary complex vector space and x,y,h are its complex-linear Chevalley actions. H003 retains finite dimension, all three commutators, a highest vector of weight λ and irreducibility under the generating actions; this is the operator presentation of the given sl₂-module, with the specified v retained separately in H004. λ remains an integer. Under H002, Index λ = Fin(λ.toNat+1) identifies bijectively with {i∈ℤ | 0≤i≤λ}; natural differences are used only inside this range. M is a ℤ-submodule of V; H005 supplies an arbitrary integer basis that is also a complex basis, and stability under every divided power. R is the ℤ-subalgebra of ℚ generated by the displayed prime-to-p fractions, with its exact original carrier established by T005; it is not assumed to be the whole ℚ. MR=R⊗ℤM and iota is the actual scalar multiplication lift; identifying with its image is justified only by N002. J_i and J are preimages of that image under c↦c e_i. qmul is the actual multiplication map ℚ⊗R J→ℂ. Scalar-first VK=K⊗ℤM is the same source tensor construction with factors swapped; T014 uses this convention throughout. ModN(MR,p) is the quotient by the image of multiplication by p. LatticeOps carries the actual restrictions, and Agrees specifies each value, rather than adding any action assumption. The target constructs these unique restrictions and states nonzero VK and every K-submodule invariant under x,y,h is bottom or top. Proposition-valued definitions only package displayed obligations; none proves them. Endomorphism multiplication is composition, integer coefficients are cast to the indicated field, and all factorials/choose arguments are natural numbers. Inspected pinned APIs: CharP K p asserts exactly (n:K)=0 iff p divides n, rather than merely p·1=0. Module.End multiplication is f.comp g, and Basis.coord is the coefficient function from its finitely-supported coordinate equivalence. LinearMap.liftBaseChange sends s⊗m to s•f(m), while LinearMap.baseChange sends s⊗m to s⊗f(m). ModN is the quotient by the range of the integer-linear p-multiplication map and its ZMod-module instance is canonical. ZMod.algebra is explicitly installed under CharP; the R-action on K is explicitly the one supplied by κ.toAlgebra. Scalar towers for R⊂ℚ⊂ℂ and R⊂ℂ acting on V use those actual inclusion maps. The two finrank theorems require strong rank condition; here R is a subring of ℚ and ℚ is a field, so these are consequences, not extra parameters.","steps":[{"id":"T001","statement":"Lowering lam x y h v","inputs":["H002","H003","H004"],"source_steps":["S001"],"explanation":"Simultaneous natural induction using the three commutators. Natural t and t+1 encode the source nonnegative and positive integer powers.","background_notes":[]},{"id":"T002","statement":"∀ i : Index lam, e y v i.val ≠ 0 ∧ h (e y v i.val) = (((lam - 2 * (i.val : ℤ) : ℤ) : ℂ) • e y v i.val)","inputs":["H002","H004","T001"],"source_steps":["S002"],"explanation":"Iterate the x identity, obtaining i! times the descending product; all factors are nonzero over ℂ for i≤λ. Divide by i! and retain the weight formula.","background_notes":[]},{"id":"T003","statement":"CoordinateCore lam x y h v","inputs":["H002","H003","T001","T002"],"source_steps":["S003","S004"],"explanation":"Isolate coefficients by products of h minus the other complex weights, use dimension to obtain the e basis, exclude the additional weight −λ−2, and derive terminated generator strings. No reduced-weight assertion is used.","background_notes":["R001.BG001"]},{"id":"T004","statement":"Coordinates lam x y h v","inputs":["H002","T001","T002","T003"],"source_steps":["S001","S005"],"explanation":"Export all of N001, including the count, lowering identities, nonzero vectors, basis, termination, and integral binomial divided-power formulas. Iterate the generator formulas and use zero-extended families at the ends.","background_notes":["R001.BG002"]},{"id":"T005","statement":"(∀ q : ℚ, q ∈ localRing p ↔ ∃ a b : ℤ, b ≠ 0 ∧ ¬ (p : ℤ) ∣ b ∧ q = (a : ℚ) / (b : ℚ)) ∧ IsFractionRing (R p) ℚ","inputs":["H001"],"source_steps":["S006","S007","S012"],"explanation":"Representation bridge for D007: the displayed fractions are closed under ring operations when p is prime, so their ℤ-adjoin has exactly that carrier. Since it contains ℤ and embeds in ℚ, ℚ is its fraction field. This establishes the concrete localization meaning before using its denominators.","background_notes":["R002.BG001","R002.BG002","R004.BG001"]},{"id":"T006","statement":"LocalLattice p lam M x y h","inputs":["H001","H002","H003","H005","T004"],"source_steps":["S006"],"explanation":"Extend an arbitrary admissible integer basis to R; compare its image in V to prove iota injective, free rank n, and full complex span. Use stability under divided powers and h=xy−yx; construct, rather than assume, the integer lattice restrictions LatticeOps.","background_notes":["R002.BG001"]},{"id":"T007","statement":"LocalLattice p lam M x y h ∧ MatrixIdentities p lam M x y v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T005","T006"],"source_steps":["S007","S008"],"explanation":"Export N002. Evaluate Pi and the two edge products on the e basis. Use λ<p to prove the binomial coefficient a unit in R, compose to obtain every matrix unit, and deduce preservation. Endomorphism multiplication is composition with the rightmost map acting first.","background_notes":["R002.BG002"]},{"id":"T008","statement":"∀ i : Index lam, Ji p M y v i.val = J p M y v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T007"],"source_steps":["S009"],"explanation":"Matrix units transport c e_j to c e_i; reverse i,j to prove equality. The zero index is present because H002 identifies Fin(λ.toNat+1) with the source interval.","background_notes":[]},{"id":"T009","statement":"CommonModule p lam M y v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T007","T008"],"source_steps":["S010","S011"],"explanation":"Export N003. The finite sum of J coordinates gives the displayed R-linear isomorphism with MR, using iota injectivity and the complex e basis. Diagonal images of finitely many generators make J finite; full span makes it nonzero.","background_notes":["R003.BG001"]},{"id":"T010","statement":"Function.Injective (qmul p M y v) ∧ (qmul p M y v).range = Submodule.span ℚ (J p M y v : Set ℂ) ∧ Module.Finite ℚ (ℚ ⊗[R p] J p M y v)","inputs":["H001","H002","T005","T009"],"source_steps":["S012"],"explanation":"Clear a common nonzero denominator in a finite tensor sum. Its image is c/d, so vanishing forces c=0. Finite generation of J yields finite-dimensional rational scalar extension.","background_notes":["R004.BG001"]},{"id":"T011","statement":"RationalLine p M y v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T005","T007","T009","T010"],"source_steps":["S013"],"explanation":"Tensor the explicit coordinate isomorphism with ℚ. Use Module.finrank_baseChange on MR and Module.finrank_pi_fintype on the finite constant family QJ directly, with the actual freeness/finiteness conditions, to obtain n=nr and hence r=1. The balanced coordinate inverse avoids a flatness assumption.","background_notes":["R004.BG002","R004.BG003"]},{"id":"T012","statement":"∃ b : ℂ, Principal p M y v b","inputs":["H001","H002","T005","T009","T011"],"source_steps":["S014","S015"],"explanation":"Choose a nonzero rational-line vector and finitely many nonzero generators. Write each rational coefficient as p^eta times a unit of R and choose the minimum exponent. Prove both inclusions J=Rb; no PID shortcut replaces this supplied argument.","background_notes":["R004.BG004","R004.BG005"]},{"id":"T013","statement":"PrincipalLattice p lam M y v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T007","T009","T011","T012"],"source_steps":["S016"],"explanation":"Export N004, including rational multiplication injectivity, rational span and rank one, existence of b, and an R-basis for every nonzero b generating J. Construct the basis using the common-module coordinate isomorphism.","background_notes":[]},{"id":"T014","statement":"ScalarReduction p K M","inputs":["H001","T005","T007"],"source_steps":["S017","S018"],"explanation":"Construct κ by fractions, then the two canonical scalar-first tensor isomorphisms by the source pure-tensor formulas and their inverses. ModN(MR,p) is literally MR/pMR and has its canonical ZMod p action. Prove naturality for lattice endomorphisms; the intermediate R and ZMod algebra structures are supplied explicitly inside TensorMaps.","background_notes":["R005.BG001"]},{"id":"T015","statement":"ReducedBasis p lam K M x y h v","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T007","T013","T014"],"source_steps":["S019"],"explanation":"Choose b from N004, scalar-extend its R-basis, and pull it back through the natural tensor isomorphism. Keep the exact basis/image equation, then transfer the integer generator matrices and dimension.","background_notes":["R005.BG002"]},{"id":"T016","statement":"ScalarReduction p K M ∧ ReducedBasis p lam K M x y h v ∧ ReducedUnits lam K M x y h ∧ ∃ a : LatticeOps M, Agrees M x y h a","inputs":["H001","H002","T004","T007","T013","T014","T015"],"source_steps":["S020"],"explanation":"Export N005. Prove all factorials through λ and all relevant binomials nonzero in K. Extend r!X_r=x^r and r!Y_r=y^r, reduce the original matrix-unit identity through the same scaled basis, and obtain the delta action. Indices are natural differences guarded by i,j≤λ. Its delta formula is stated for every basis with the generator formulas, so the very same beta exported by ReducedBasis satisfies all N005 assertions.","background_notes":["R005.BG003"]},{"id":"T017","statement":"∀ (a : LatticeOps M) (W : Submodule K (VK K M)), Agrees M x y h a → Invariant W (a.x.baseChange K) → Invariant W (a.y.baseChange K) → Invariant W (a.h.baseChange K) → ∀ i j : Index lam, Invariant W (MatrixWord lam (fun r => (a.X r).baseChange K) (fun r => (a.Y r).baseChange K) i j)","inputs":["H002","T016"],"source_steps":["S021"],"explanation":"Powers of preserving x,y preserve W. For every index in MatrixWord, N005 identifies the divided powers with scalar multiples of those powers; scalar multiplication and composition preserve W.","background_notes":["R006.BG001"]},{"id":"T018","statement":"∀ a : LatticeOps M, Agrees M x y h a → Simple (a.x.baseChange K) (a.y.baseChange K) (a.h.baseChange K)","inputs":["T016","T017"],"source_steps":["S022","S023","S024"],"explanation":"Use the N005 beta basis and separate zero/nonzero W. In the nonzero case choose a single compatible vector, coefficient family, and nonzero coordinate; apply the matrix word and divide to put every beta_i in W. Basis cardinality λ.toNat+1 supplies nontriviality. All temporary witnesses remain local.","background_notes":["R006.BG002","R006.BG003"]},{"id":"T019","statement":"Target K M x y h","inputs":["H001","H002","H003","T016","T018"],"source_steps":["S025"],"explanation":"Choose the actual lattice restrictions carried with N005 and apply T018. These are N002's already constructed restrictions transported through N005, not an additional original premise. Agrees pins their values uniquely on M. Nontriviality and the zero/top invariant-subspace alternative are the complete target; invariance under the three Chevalley generators is equivalent to invariance under their K-span, including characteristic two.","background_notes":["R006.BG004"]}],"terminal":"T019"},"has_more":false,"next_offset":null}
