{"id":"background-supplement.json","pointer":"","view":"value","value":{"policy":"mathematical-background-v2-1","graph_sha256":"871c56f88a6aa1287475358282eb2cc7e3debe52ab662e33aa5f88612cc67d78","spec_sha256":"ddf45cbfe95004e05c6e57612b423e68b8210d2f6a8b0bc3ea97a3e66f137f05","notes":{"R001":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"In a finite-dimensional vector space W over a field, a linearly independent family indexed by a finite set J with |J|=dim W is a basis.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"V is a complex vector space of finite dimension λ+1."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement","reason":"F001 gives |I|=n=λ+1."},{"location":"/inference/argument/2/text","reason":"Before the dimension step, the displayed polynomial calculation proves linear independence of (e_i)."}],"justification":"The elementary exchange argument bounds the size of any independent family by the size of a finite spanning family: insert independent vectors successively into a spanning list, each time replacing a vector with nonzero coefficient in an expansion, preserving spanning. If an independent family of size dim W did not span, adjoining a vector outside its span would give an independent family of size dim W+1, contradicting that bound applied to a basis of W. Thus the given family spans and is a basis."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/4/text","fact":"For integers m≥r≥0, m!/(r!(m−r)!) is an integer, denoted binom(m,r). Consequently, for integers a≥0 and r≥0, (a+1)⋯(a+r)/r!=binom(a+r,r) is an integer, with empty product 1.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"λ is a nonnegative integer."},{"location":"/definitions/1/definition","reason":"i∈I means 0≤i≤λ, so both a=λ−i and a=i are nonnegative integers."},{"location":"/inference/locals/5","reason":"r is an arbitrary natural number."},{"location":"/inference/argument/4/text","reason":"The two computed successive products use a=λ−i and a=i, respectively."}],"justification":"The factorial quotients equal 1 at r=0 and r=m. For 0<r<m, direct common-denominator arithmetic gives C(m,r)=C(m−1,r−1)+C(m−1,r). Induction on m therefore proves every such quotient integral. Cancelling a! in (a+r)!/a! gives the stated successive-product expression. Interpreting these integers in ℂ gives exactly the coefficients in S005; all factorial denominators are nonzero."}],"R002":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/0/text","fact":"If (m_i) indexed by the finite set I is an integer basis of M and also a complex basis of V, then R⊗_ℤ M has R-basis (1⊗m_i). The map r⊗m↦rm into V is injective, has image the R-span of M, and its image spans V over ℂ. Here |I|=n, so its R-rank is n.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"H005 supplies an integer basis of M that is a complex basis of V."},{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"N001 supplies the complex basis (e_i) indexed by I and |I|=n, so finite-dimensional basis cardinality gives the same indexing size for m."},{"location":"/definitions/6","reason":"R is a subring of ℚ⊂ℂ under H001."},{"location":"/definitions/7","reason":"M_R is the stated tensor product."},{"location":"/definitions/8","reason":"iota is the canonical scalar-multiplication map."}],"justification":"Finite basis exchange gives equality of the cardinalities of the two complex bases. Write a_i:M→ℤ for the integer coordinate maps for m. The maps r⊗m↦r a_i(m) are well-defined by bilinearity and give an inverse to (r_i)↦∑r_i⊗m_i. The map into V sends this sum to ∑r_i m_i, which vanishes only if every coefficient vanishes, by complex independence and R⊂ℂ. Its image is precisely the finite R-linear combinations of M, contains each m_i, and hence has complex span V. This elaborates the coordinate construction already displayed in S001."},{"id":"BG002","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/1/text","fact":"For a prime p and integers 0≤j≤λ<p, the integer c=binom(λ,j) is not divisible by p and is a unit in R=ℤ_(p), with inverse 1/c∈R.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"p is prime."},{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"0≤λ<p."},{"location":"/definitions/1","reason":"j∈I means 0≤j≤λ."},{"location":"/definitions/6","reason":"A rational with denominator not divisible by p belongs to R."},{"location":"/inference/argument/1/text","reason":"S002 uses the integer identity c j!(λ−j)!=λ!."}],"justification":"Each positive factor in these factorials is less than p and therefore coprime to p; the Euclidean algorithm gives an inverse for its residue modulo p. Products of these invertible residues remain invertible, including the empty product 1. Thus p does not divide λ!. If p divided c, the displayed integer identity would force p to divide λ!, a contradiction. Consequently c≠0 and the fraction 1/c satisfies the defining denominator condition for R; its product with c is 1."}],"R003":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/2/text","fact":"For the R-module L=iota(M_R), the image E_ii(L)=J e_i is finitely generated: L has a finite generating family because M_R is free of finite rank n and iota is an R-linear isomorphism onto L. Moreover c↦c e_i is an R-linear isomorphism J→J e_i, so J is finitely generated.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/3/statement","reason":"M_R is free of finite rank n and iota embeds it in V."},{"location":"/definitions/8/definition","reason":"iota is R-linear."},{"location":"/inference/argument/0/produces/0","reason":"F001 identifies E_ii(L) with J e_i."},{"location":"/inputs/2/statement","reason":"Each e_i is a nonzero vector of a complex basis."},{"location":"/inputs/1/statement","reason":"0≤λ gives 0∈I, so an index i can be taken, including when λ=0."}],"justification":"Choose a finite basis b_1,…,b_n of M_R from its finite freeness. Their images generate L. By R-linearity, E_ii(∑ r_a iota(b_a))=∑ r_a E_ii(iota(b_a)), so these finitely many projected images generate J e_i. Scalar multiplication J→J e_i is surjective by the definition of J e_i and injective since e_i≠0 in a complex vector space. Transporting the projected generators through its inverse yields a finite generating family of J."}],"R004":[{"id":"BG001","kind":"mathematical","at":"/inference/argument/0/text","fact":"For R=ℤ_(p)⊂ℚ and an R-submodule J⊂ℂ, every element of ℚ⊗_R J has the form d⁻¹⊗c with 0≠d∈R and c∈J. Multiplication is a well-defined injective ℚ-linear map with image span_ℚ(J). If J is finitely generated over R, the images of those generators span the tensor product over ℚ.","grounds":[{"location":"/inputs/0/statement","reason":"p is prime, so D007 defines the stated subring."},{"location":"/definitions/5","reason":"R contains ℤ and is contained in ℚ."},{"location":"/inputs/4/statement","reason":"J is an R-submodule of ℂ and finitely generated."}],"justification":"For a finite sum ∑ρ_j⊗c_j choose a common nonzero integer denominator d and integers a_j=dρ_j. Balancing gives ∑ρ_j⊗c_j=d⁻¹⊗∑a_jc_j. Multiplication is balanced because the R actions are scalar multiplication in ℂ. If its image is zero, c/d=0 in ℂ forces c=0. Its image consists precisely of rational linear combinations, and an R-generating expression for c gives the asserted finite rational …12494 tokens truncated…eld of characteristic \\(p\\), and\n17: \\(\\lambda\\) an integer with \\(0\\leq\\lambda<p\\).\n18: Let \\(V(\\lambda)\\) be the complex irreducible highest-weight\n19: \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2(\\mathbb C)\\)-module of highest weight \\(\\lambda\\),\n20: of dimension \\(\\lambda+1\\). Its Chevalley generators satisfy\n21: \n22: \\[\n23: [h,x]=2x,\\qquad[h,y]=-2y,\\qquad[x,y]=h.\n24: \\]\n25: \n26: Choose a nonzero highest-weight vector \\(v\\) with \\(xv=0\\) and\n27: \\(hv=\\lambda v\\). Let \\(U_{\\mathbb Z}\\) be the integer subalgebra of\n28: the universal enveloping algebra generated by all divided powers\n29: \\(X_r=x^r/r!\\), \\(Y_r=y^r/r!\\), for integers \\(r\\geq0\\).\n30: An **admissible lattice** \\(M\\subset V(\\lambda)\\) is a free abelian\n31: subgroup with an integer basis that is also a complex basis, stable under\n32: \\(U_{\\mathbb Z}\\). It is arbitrary, not necessarily the minimal lattice.\n33: \n34: Put \\(V(K)=M\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}K\\). The Lie algebra\n35: \\(L(K)=(\\mathbb Zx+\\mathbb Zy+\\mathbb Zh)\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}K\\)\n36: acts by the induced lattice operators. This is the Chevalley\n37: \\(\\mathfrak{sl}_2\\) construction also in characteristic 2.\n38: Prove that \\(V(K)\\) is nonzero and every \\(K\\)-linear subspace\n39: invariant under \\(x,y,h\\) is zero or all of \\(V(K)\\).\n40: Both \\(\\lambda=0\\) and \\(p=2\\) are included; \\(K\\) need not be\n41: algebraically closed.\n42: \n43: ## Fixed natural-language proof\n44: \n45: ### 1. Divided-power coordinates in characteristic zero\n46: \n47: Put \\(n=\\lambda+1\\) and \\(e_i=y^iv/i!\\) for \\(0\\leq i\\leq\\lambda\\).\n48: The commutator identities give, by induction,\n49: \n50: \\[\n51: hy^iv=(\\lambda-2i)y^iv,\\qquad\n52: xy^iv=i(\\lambda-i+1)y^{i-1}v\\quad(i\\geq1).\n53: \\]\n54: \n55: For \\(i\\leq\\lambda\\), applying \\(x^i\\) to \\(y^iv\\) gives\n56: \\(i!\\lambda(\\lambda-1)\\cdots(\\lambda-i+1)v\\ne0\\) over \\(\\mathbb C\\).\n57: At \\(i=0\\) the empty product is one. Thus every \\(e_i\\ne0\\).\n58: Their \\(h\\)-eigenvalues \\(\\lambda-2i\\) are distinct over \\(\\mathbb C\\),\n59: so they are linearly independent: applying\n60: \\(\\prod_{k\\ne j}(h-(\\lambda-2k)\\mathrm{Id})\\) to a relation isolates\n61: its \\(j\\)-th coefficient with a nonzero multiplier.\n62: There are \\(n\\) vectors, the dimension of \\(V(\\lambda)\\), hence they\n63: form a basis. Also \\(y^{\\lambda+1}v=0\\), since otherwise it would be\n64: an eigenvector of eigenvalue \\(-\\lambda-2\\), absent from this basis.\n65: All higher lowering powers vanish as well.\n66: \n67: The operator matrices are therefore\n68: \n69: \\[\n70: xe_0=0,\\quad xe_i=(\\lambda-i+1)e_{i-1}\\ (i\\geq1),\\qquad\n71: ye_i=(i+1)e_{i+1}\\ (i<\\lambda),\\quad ye_\\lambda=0,\n72: \\quad he_i=(\\lambda-2i)e_i.\n73: \\]\n74: \n75: Iteration gives the integral divided-power matrices\n76: \n77: \\[\n78: X_re_i=\\binom{\\lambda-i+r}{r}e_{i-r}\\quad(0\\leq r\\leq i),\n79: \\qquad\n80: Y_re_i=\\binom{i+r}{r}e_{i+r}\\quad(i+r\\leq\\lambda).       \\tag{1}\n81: \\]\n82: \n83: The respective expressions are zero when \\(r>i\\) or\n84: \\(i+r>\\lambda\\), by termination of the strings just proved.\n85: \n86: ### 2. Matrix units preserve the localized arbitrary lattice\n87: \n88: Let \\(R=\\mathbb Z_{(p)}=\\{a/b\\in\\mathbb Q:p\\nmid b\\}\\), and regard\n89: \\(M_R=R\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}M\\) as a submodule of \\(V(\\lambda)\\).\n90: The embedding is injective because an integer basis of \\(M\\) is\n91: complex-linearly independent. The module \\(M_R\\) is free of rank \\(n\\)\n92: over \\(R\\) and is preserved by all \\(X_r,Y_r\\), by admissibility.\n93: \n94: Define \\(E_{ij}\\) to send \\(e_j\\) to \\(e_i\\) and all other basis\n95: vectors to zero. Formula (1) gives\n96: \n97: \\[\n98: P:=Y_\\lambda X_\\lambda=E_{\\lambda\\lambda},\\qquad\n99: X_{\\lambda-i}P=E_{i\\lambda},\\qquad\n100: P Y_{\\lambda-j}=\\binom\\lambda j E_{\\lambda j}.\n101: \\]\n102: \n103: For the last identity, an input index below \\(j\\) is sent below\n104: \\(e_\\lambda\\) and killed by \\(P\\); an index above \\(j\\) is sent past\n105: the end of the lowering string. On \\(e_j\\) the coefficient is precisely\n106: \\(\\binom\\lambda j\\). Since \\(\\lambda<p\\), none of\n107: \\(\\lambda!\\), \\(j!\\), \\((\\lambda-j)!\\) is divisible by \\(p\\), so\n108: \\(\\binom\\lambda j\\) is a unit in \\(R\\). Consequently\n109: \n110: \\[\n111: E_{ij}=\\left(\\binom\\lambda j\\right)^{-1}\n112:        X_{\\lambda-i}P Y_{\\lambda-j}.                 \\tag{2}\n113: \\]\n114: \n115: Every matrix unit preserves \\(M_R\\). No assertion about distinct\n116: weights modulo \\(p\\) has been used.\n117: \n118: ### 3. Recover a standard basis for the arbitrary lattice\n119: \n120: Define \\(J_i=\\{c\\in\\mathbb C:c e_i\\in M_R\\}\\).\n121: The coordinate projection \\(E_{ii}\\) maps \\(M_R\\) onto \\(J_i e_i\\).\n122: Applying \\(E_{ij}\\) to \\(c e_j\\) shows \\(J_j\\subseteq J_i\\);\n123: reversing the indices gives equality. Let \\(J\\) be their common module.\n124: It is finitely generated, being a projection of a finite free module,\n125: and nonzero because \\(M_R\\) spans \\(V(\\lambda)\\) over \\(\\mathbb C\\).\n126: The coordinate projections and independence of the \\(e_i\\) give\n127: \n128: \\[\n129: M_R=\\bigoplus_{i=0}^{\\lambda}J e_i.                  \\tag{3}\n130: \\]\n131: \n132: One must still prove that \\(J\\) has rank one; containment in\n133: \\(\\mathbb C\\) alone does not imply that. Let\n134: \\(r=\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}(\\mathbb Q\\otimes_R J)\\), which is finite.\n135: Tensoring (3) with the fraction field \\(\\mathbb Q\\) and using the\n136: rank \\(n\\) of \\(M_R\\) gives \\(n=nr\\), so \\(r=1\\), since \\(n>0\\).\n137: The multiplication map \\(\\mathbb Q\\otimes_R J\\to\\mathbb C\\) is\n138: injective: this tensor product is the localization of the torsion-free\n139: module \\(J\\subset\\mathbb C\\), and a fraction mapping to zero has\n140: zero numerator. Thus \\(J\\) spans a one-dimensional rational line.\n141: \n142: Choose \\(0\\ne a\\in J\\) and write a finite set of nonzero generators\n143: as \\(q_1a,\\ldots,q_ka\\) with \\(q_j\\in\\mathbb Q^\\times\\).\n144: Each \\(q_j=p^{\\nu_j}u_j\\) for \\(\\nu_j\\in\\mathbb Z\\) and a unit\n145: \\(u_j\\) of \\(R\\). Let \\(\\nu=\\min_j\\nu_j\\). All generators belong\n146: to \\(p^\\nu R a\\), and a generator with minimal exponent generates\n147: that whole module. Therefore \\(J=Rb\\) for a nonzero \\(b\\in J\\).\n148: By (3), \\(b e_0,\\ldots,b e_\\lambda\\) is an \\(R\\)-basis of \\(M_R\\).\n149: \n150: ### 4. Reduce and extend scalars\n151: \n152: All denominators in \\(R\\) are invertible in \\(K\\), so the integer map\n153: extends to \\(R\\to K\\). The natural tensor maps give\n154: \n155: \\[\n156: M\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}K\\cong M_R\\otimes_R K\n157: \\cong(M_R/pM_R)\\otimes_{\\mathbb F_p}K.\n158: \\]\n159: \n160: For example the first map sends \\(m\\otimes c\\) to\n161: \\((1\\otimes m)\\otimes c\\); its inverse sends\n162: \\((r\\otimes m)\\otimes c\\) to \\(m\\otimes\\bar r c\\).\n163: The reduced images \\(b_0,\\ldots,b_\\lambda\\) of \\(b e_i\\) form a\n164: \\(K\\)-basis, with exactly the matrices of step 1 reduced into \\(K\\).\n165: The matrix-unit identities (2) persist on this basis.\n166: \n167: For \\(0\\leq r\\leq\\lambda<p\\), \\(r!\\ne0\\) in \\(K\\), and the induced\n168: \\(X_r,Y_r\\) equal \\((r!)^{-1}x^r,(r!)^{-1}y^r\\). Hence any\n169: \\(K\\)-subspace invariant under \\(x,y\\) is invariant under every matrix\n170: unit in (2).\n171: \n172: ### 5. Conclude irreducibility\n173: \n174: Let \\(W\\) be invariant. If it is nonzero, choose\n175: \\(0\\ne w=\\sum_i c_i b_i\\in W\\), and an index \\(j\\) with \\(c_j\\ne0\\).\n176: For every \\(i\\), \\(E_{ij}w=c_jb_i\\in W\\). Division by \\(c_j\\)\n177: gives \\(b_i\\in W\\), so all basis vectors lie in \\(W\\) and\n178: \\(W=V(K)\\). Since \\(\\dim_K V(K)=\\lambda+1>0\\), it is nonzero and\n179: irreducible. If \\(\\lambda=0\\), all relevant factorials and binomial\n180: coefficients are one and the argument applies unchanged. For \\(p=2\\),"}
{"path": "nl02lean/references/audit-b.md", "total_lines": 175, "start": 1, "end": 175, "start_column": 0, "end_column": 74, "requested_end": 250, "has_more": false, "next_start": null, "next_column": 0, "next_end": null, "text": "1: # Audit B: endpoint fidelity and checked Lean closure\n2: \n3: The stage-two policy is `endpoint-fidelity-2` (Audit B schema version 2).\n4: Its semantic objects are every original external theorem E and the complete\n5: final target P. P includes its parameters, quantifiers, hypotheses, domains and\n6: conclusion. Accepted NL0/Audit A remains the source authority; B does not repeat\n7: unchanged natural-language proof checking.\n8: \n9: ## Normal final acceptance and deviation exception\n10: \n11: The normal workflow uses `yui-frozen-interface-acceptance-1`. A complete independent\n12: pre-proof review establishes source-to-type fidelity for every E and full P and\n13: all definitions needed to interpret them. Final native inspection compares the\n14: actual exported full types against that frozen contract, checks the exact defining\n15: header/pins and semantic receipt, and validates standalone proof/dependency/trust\n16: closure. This normal final gate is pure machine work: no additional final LLM is\n17: called. It records inherited semantics, not a newly performed independent Audit B.\n18: \n19: The actual E/P types include retained hypotheses and quantifiers. Text labels or\n20: a source-hash check alone do not establish this gate. The original mathematical\n21: review may be wrong; the machine establishes preservation of that reviewed encoding,\n22: not correctness of the reviewer's natural-language judgment.\n23: \n24: Only complete proof-integrity success plus genuine endpoint/definition drift may\n25: open the exceptional independent B-final review below. A faithful result then has\n26: `AUDIT-B-CONDITIONAL`, a freeze-deviation warning and an explicit harness-defect\n27: report. Invalid native evidence, incomplete dependencies or changed provenance\n28: cannot be overridden semantically. Nonfaithful/unverified results wait for the\n29: three explicit user choices in [proof repair](proof-repair.md); accepting a different\n30: result does not complete the original target. Explicit/manual legacy semantic\n31: receipts remain supported under their original schemas and are not relabeled.\n32: Explicitly nonindependent/self-review records remain readable development checks;\n33: they cannot grant whole-workflow Audit B acceptance. The same qualification rule\n34: applies to supplied Audit A authority and incremental producer/cut reviews.\n35: \n36: ## Author correspondence explanations\n37: \n38: During interface authoring, provide `endpoint_correspondence`, keyed by every\n39: E ID and P. Each entry is `{mode: direct|equivalent, explanation: string}`.\n40: Keep it short and useful; do not restate the entire proof or manufacture a\n41: line-by-line formalization commentary:\n42: \n43: - **Direct:** identify the corresponding variables, hypotheses and assertion.\n44:   One sentence is enough when the translation is immediate.\n45: - **Equivalent:** explain the mathematical identification or reformulation,\n46:   why it works in both directions and which existing conditions it uses.\n47:   Point to relevant definitions or checked conversions when needed.\n48: \n49: For example, expressing invariance under a finite basis of operators can encode\n50: invariance under their linear span: explain the spanning property and closure\n51: of a subspace under linear combinations. The review must establish those facts\n52: for the actual source algebra/action; the word “equivalent” alone is insufficient.\n53: \n54: The postprocessing author carries these notes into each E/P entry in\n55: `mapping.json`, checks them against the actual export and completes missing\n56: notes for older frozen contracts. It must not change frozen proof statements\n57: or bodies merely to improve a note. An explanation is an author's claim, not\n58: an Audit B verdict or a formal proof.\n59: \n60: ## Semantic responsibility before freeze or at explicit B-final\n61: \n62: Read the explanation together with the original source statement and actual\n63: elaborated Lean type. Follow the definitions needed to interpret the endpoint,\n64: including structure fields that might hide extra assumptions. Check each E/P\n65: row, then these four global checks:\n66: \n67: 1. `endpoint_inventory_and_scope`: all original E statements and P are covered;\n68:    the source version, reviewer scope and inherited qualifications are explicit.\n69: 2. `external_statement_fidelity`: each external assumption has the source's\n70:    complete mathematical meaning, including conditions and quantifiers. With\n71:    no external inputs, record that fact.\n72: 3. `complete_target_and_representations`: the entire target theorem has the\n73:    same meaning, including every hypothesis. Validate the explanation of each\n74:    direct or equivalent encoding and relevant edge cases.\n75: 4. `kernel_closure_and_trust`: the actual exported proof compiled, all required\n76:    producers and intermediate library ports were discharged, and the checked\n77:    root has only the declared external assumptions and permitted foundations.\n78: \n79: Equivalent formulations are allowed. Do not reject a faithful encoding because\n80: it lacks a particular Mathlib type/name, a separate object for every NL0\n81: D node, or a duplicate formal representation. A mathematical source-to-code\n82: identification can be checked semantically without constructing an unnecessary\n83: second model. If the implementation actually crosses two different Lean\n84: representations, the required conversion must occur in checked Lean code.\n85: Unexplained equivalence, stronger assumptions, missing hypotheses, changed\n86: domains or hidden target assumptions remain defects.\n87: \n88: Do not accept an explanation on trust or copy the author's conclusion into the\n89: review. Record PASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED with specific findings and source/type/note\n90: locations. A compiler pass cannot substitute for this independent judgment.\n91: \n92: ## Freely available Mathlib\n93: \n94: All proved results in the pinned Mathlib environment are available in stage\n95: two. Do not compare their names, counts or node-by-node uses with the NL0 M\n96: inventory. There is no per-M semantic row, imported-proof whitelist,\n97: `calculation_links` ledger, or separate review of implementation N/D nodes.\n98: Definitions are examined when they determine endpoint meaning, not for an\n99: exact inventory match. Notes from Audit A are optional local guidance, not\n100: permissions to use a library theorem.\n101: \n102: New interfaces normally use Mathlib directly. An older or deliberately\n103: conditional interface can retain M ports; `library_terms` supplies an actual\n104: Lean proof for each such port at assembly. These are discharge proofs, not a\n105: library correspondence inventory. They cannot remain unproved assumptions of\n106: the final export. New external axioms or stronger project hypotheses still\n107: require returning to the affected source boundary.\n108: \n109: ## Mechanical evidence and limits\n110: \n111: ### Compact review materials\n112: \n113: The semantic reviewer reads the separately versioned\n114: `yui-audit-b-review-view-1` in `review-packet.json`. Start at `/scope` and\n115: `/mechanical_summary`, then examine each `/endpoints/<ID>` source, actual\n116: inspected type and author correspondence. P retains the complete original\n117: target, parameters and hypotheses. The view is a deterministic Python extraction,\n118: not an LLM summary or mathematical judgment. It never substitutes a declared\n119: expected type for the actual inspected type.\n120: \n121: `/documents` identifies hash-bound `source-context.json`, `source-original.txt`\n122: and actual `Proof.lean`. Read relevant original and Lean definitions there,\n123: including hidden structure fields, when needed to interpret an endpoint.\n124: Complete raw compiler, elaboration and dependency records stay in the machine\n125: archive; they are not default reviewer inputs. A mechanical summary reports\n126: actual checks and exceptions, without populating a semantic PASS. The full\n127: dependency inspection remains a program responsibility.\n128: \n129: Use `read_task_input` JSON pointers, not guessed fixed line positions. Large\n130: containers expose keys and large strings expose `next_offset`. Text reads\n131: resume with `next_start` and `next_column`. Serialized read results are at most\n132: 32 KiB; follow `has_more` until the needed statement is complete. This pagination\n133: limit is not a task time/token/call budget. Unknown calls or modified feedback\n134: remain rejected with a specific local protocol fault, not a retryable HTTP 502.\n135: \n136: ### Explicit reuse of completed native evidence\n137: \n138: `audit_b.py reuse-review <new-directory> --original <old-audit-directory>`\n139: prepares a new semantic review without rebuilding or changing Lean. It validates\n140: every original input and snapshot, the original native artifacts/output, source\n141: and accepted-A authority, environment pins, and the exact historical tool\n142: snapshots. The new view and current reviewer runtime are separately bound in\n143: `review-reuse.json`; the normal current-build tool check is not disabled.\n144: Changed or incomplete evidence blocks reuse. Preserve the original failure.\n145: \n146: The new independent reviewer receives no previous B verdict, reviewer history,\n147: owner reasoning or other-case material. The receipt discloses inherited native\n148: checks and a new semantic review; it cannot claim fresh compilation, an unaided\n149: cold whole-workflow success or measured acceleration. Changing proofs or source\n150: requires the appropriate new checks, rather than this review-only path.\n151: \n152: `audit_b.py prepare/build/check` binds the source, accepted A, contract, plan,\n153: proofs, tool versions and environment hashes. Build compiles standalone\n154: `Proof.lean` before adding inspection. It checks E/P against their declared\n155: encoded types, modulo definitional equality and bijective universe renaming.\n156: It does not prove equivalence between natural-language text and Lean.\n157: Distinct universes must not be collapsed; changed types or hypotheses must not\n158: be normalized away. Source fidelity is established by the bound pre-proof independent review, or an actual exceptional B-final review; this native comparison does not establish it anew.\n159: \n160: Inspection follows the actual `AuditBExport.P` closure. No `sorry`, unproved\n161: local axiom/opaque substitute or undeclared foundation may be introduced.\n162: Local bodies still compile against their own task inputs and the pinned\n163: library; every required producer must exist. Assembly, inspection and semantic\n164: review have separate recorded outcomes.\n165: \n166: Version-1 receipts remain historical results under their original policy.\n167: They cannot be silently relabeled or accepted as version-2 reviews. Start a\n168: new bound audit when policy, source, code or evidence changes. Pending templates\n169: never pass. A whole accepted source plus complete native gates and its bound semantic\n170: authority yields `AUDIT-B-PASSED`; a fragment yields at most the explicitly\n171: scoped development verdict. A whole source with an actual conditional Audit A\n172: can yield `AUDIT-B-CONDITIONAL` after all the same native and semantic checks.\n173: It retains the exact source warnings and never upgrades them to full PASS.\n174: Neither proves external publications in Lean or\n175: establishes formalizer speedup. Preserve failures and report actual costs."}

